NAME
       audio_open, audio_write, audio_close, audio_mixer - sound output

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/cervus.h>

       int  cervus_audio_open(unsigned rate);
       long cervus_audio_write(const void *pcm, unsigned long len);
       int  cervus_audio_close(void);

       int  cervus_audio_mixer_get(cervus_audio_mixer_t *m);
       int  cervus_audio_set_volume(int pct);
       int  cervus_audio_set_mute(int mute);
       int  cervus_audio_set_output(int idx);

DESCRIPTION
       Plays audio through whichever driver claimed the card. The
       sample format is fixed: signed 16-bit stereo, interleaved, at
       the rate given to open. Converting from anything else is the
       caller's job.

       Writes block until the data has been queued, so a producer that
       is faster than real time simply waits. Short writes are packed
       into the device buffers rather than padded, so any length may be
       written; close flushes whatever is left and waits for it to
       drain.

       The device is released automatically when the process that
       opened it exits, so a program killed mid-playback does not leave
       the card looping.

MIXER
       cervus_audio_mixer_get fills in the driver name, the volume in
       percent, the mute flag and the list of outputs. Each output has
       a name, a kind (speaker, headphone, line out, digital) and a
       present flag driven by jack detection.

       cervus_audio_set_output takes an index, or -1 for automatic,
       which follows the jacks: headphone over line out over the
       internal speaker.

RETURN VALUE
       0 or a byte count on success, negative on failure.

ERRORS
       ENODEV   no sound card, or no usable output on the codec

SEE ALSO
       play(1), mixer(1)
